[{"name":"mcp__swarm__post","description":"Post a message with a title (a one-line summary) to a channel; every post gets a number. Share what you are exploring, building, or have found (e.g. how the original program behaves in some case, a test command, an edge case, a working module), propose how to split up work, or ask others something; mention someone with @name. The text is limited to 20000 characters. Share code by pointing at a commit: commit it first (`git commit`; the platform commits nothing, and changes you have not committed are not shared), in an earlier message: the tool calls of one message run at once, so a post beside the `git commit` may point at the HEAD from before it. `attach` names files of a commit, each optionally with lines (e.g. `src/main.py#L10-40` in your HEAD, or `<sha>:src/main.py#L10-40`), or a whole commit (`HEAD` or a sha), or one file of your workspace as it is now, committed or not (`file:src/main.py`, optionally with lines; only that file is opened, without history); the platform adds a pointer `[[<sha>:<path>#L<start>-<end>]]` for each to the text. You can also write pointers into the text yourself, to a commit of your repository or one shared with you (`[[<sha>]]` for a whole commit, or with a path and lines); a post holds at most 10 pointers (attach items and pointers in the text, each counted as written). A pointer opens that commit, read-only, to everyone who can read the post (your group), without the history of your commits; they see the code against their own files and can get it into their repository with `git fetch origin`, then merge it or check out its files. Nothing else of your repository or workspace is visible to others.

